WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.070
- Today we're gonna talk
about a bunch of methods

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.660
that I use to stop procrastinating.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.170
These are methods that I've developed

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:07.710
over the past couple of years,

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.560
and also methods that
I've heavily borrowed

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.950
from other people,
completely ripping them off,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.690
and now I'm making money
off of their ideas.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:17.230
Okay, so number one, eat the frog.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:20.240
Mark Twain said, "If it's
your job to eat a frog,

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.660
it's best to do it first
thing in the morning.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.720
And if it's your job to eat two frogs,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.910
it's best to eat the biggest one first."

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.450
Knowing that you have to eat a frog

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:31.130
is unpleasant to think about,
(frog croaking)

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:34.500
but if it's something that you
have to do every single day,

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.070
the day starts, the sun rises,

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:38.650
and you're presented with a frog

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.620
that you have to eat every single morning,

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.358
then why put it off?

00:00:42.358 --> 00:00:47.358
(ethereal choir music)
(frog sloshing)

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:53.580
Because if you put it off,

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:54.930
the whole day you're gonna be thinking

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.930
about this dreadful thing you have to do.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.800
You'll be haunted by this idea.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.900
You'll try to have a normal
conversation with other people,

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.560
you're gonna try to relax
and unwind in the bathtub,

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.600
and all you're gonna be
thinking about is the fact

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.110
that you have to shove a
slimy toad down your throat.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.040
And in this thankfully
hypothetical scenario,

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.150
if you were to put off eating
that frog until tomorrow,

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.640
you would have two frogs to eat.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.120
And if you were to follow
this pattern, day after day,

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.770
postponing eating the frog,

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:21.810
then at the end of the month,

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.800
you'll have like 30 frogs to eat.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.950
You're gonna have to have
this giant 30 frog mukbang.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:28.160
That's extremely unpleasant.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:29.800
Why would you do that to yourself?

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.230
But if it's inevitable
and you have to do it,

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.220
why not do it first thing in the morning

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.900
so that you can live the
rest of your day in peace?

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.390
So do yourself a favor

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.078
and eat that frog first
thing in the morning.

00:01:41.078 --> 00:01:42.090
(electronic beeping)

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.640
(man crunching)

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.900
Number two, the 3-2-1 rule.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.940
Now the 3-2-1 rule is pretty stupid,

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.710
but it's too stupid to ignore.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:54.810
So the way it works is, say,
you've got to write an essay

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.900
for university or for high school,

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.700
or whatever the hell you do, I don't know.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.370
You hold this thing that
you have to do in your head

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:02.243
and you reflect on it.

00:02:02.243 --> 00:02:04.310
And you say, "I've got
to go and do this thing."

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.300
And then you count from three to one.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.480
You say, "Damn, I gotta write my essay.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.340
Three, two, one."

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.370
And then you just go and do
the thing that you're avoiding.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:16.360
Now that sounds stupid and
overly simple because it is,

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.480
but it also works.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.210
And I think what's going on here is that

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:20.810
by using this strategy,

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.760
we are utilizing psychological momentum.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.280
Writing an essay seems super
scary so we put it off.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.970
But counting is easy.

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:30.000
Anyone can count.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.090
So we're way less likely
to procrastinate counting.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:36.120
But once we start counting,
we develop momentum

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.560
towards doing the thing
that we're avoiding.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.450
It's like a psychological bridge.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.470
So next time you're having
a very difficult time

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:45.530
just diving in and starting
a task you need to do,

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.920
say, "Three, two, one,"

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:48.770
then just go do it.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:50.540
I don't know why it works, but it works.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.650
Speaking of psychological momentum,

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.220
number three is the do-something
principle by Mark Manson.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.900
So most people think that
in order to take action

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.690
on something, we need to
be motivated to do it.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:03.250
And motivation comes from

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.290
some sort of emotional inspiration.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:07.260
First, we're inspired to take action.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.170
We have the strong reason to do it,

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.470
and that generates the motivation we need

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:13.010
to take action on the thing.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:16.300
But action isn't just
the effect of motivation.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.650
It's also the cause of it.

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:22.250
Inspiration, motivation, action
is not a linear sequence.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:23.840
It's an endless loop

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.110
and you can start
wherever you wanna start.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:28.150
And since inspiration and motivation

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:31.470
seem to be hard to come by
and completely unpredictable,

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.150
it's way more efficient
to start with action

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:37.380
and let that action serve
as the inspirational

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:40.670
and motivational fuel
to power further action.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:41.503
And you might be saying,

00:03:41.503 --> 00:03:43.750
"Well, how am I supposed
to take action on something

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:45.460
without inspiration and motivation?

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.180
That's the entire problem here.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.370
The problem is that I can't take action.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:51.530
I need some sort of
inspiration and motivation."

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:52.960
But that's not entirely true

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:55.220
because all of us take action on things

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.740
with seemingly little or
no inspiration whatsoever.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.030
And if we didn't, we'd be dead.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.210
We do certain things every
day that are habitual.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.540
We brush our teeth, we take a shower,

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.410
we put on nice clothes.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:09.680
These seemingly insignificant actions

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.950
are the very actions we
should harness the reaction of

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:15.640
to generate inspiration and motivation

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.110
towards doing further action.

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:18.480
It's like a snowball effect.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.780
So next time you're
avoiding doing your taxes

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:22.540
because it's super boring,

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.450
tell yourself, "Just do
something, anything,"

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:28.090
and harness the reaction from that action

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:30.880
to generate the motivation
to take further action.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:33.620
And that, in essence, is
the do-something principle.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:36.820
Number four is purge your
physical surroundings.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.700
So a lot of the reason why
so many of us procrastinate

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.600
so often is because the
physical environment

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.660
in which we live in
practically begs us to.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.840
And as we've discussed
on the channel before,

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:48.960
there's an intimate link

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.810
between our psychology and our physiology.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:56.500
If we exist in a chaotic,
disastrous physical environment,

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.750
our brain often follows suit.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:02.540
A chaotic and distracted
mental state very often creates

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.930
a chaotic and disastrous physical state,

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:08.140
which further perpetuates
the distracted mental state.

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:11.040
It's a paradox, but it's
often so much easier

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.250
to correct and improve upon
your physical environment

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:17.360
than it is to suddenly feel good

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:18.790
and clear-headed all of a sudden.

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:20.610
So if you're more distracted than ever

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:22.640
and you're finding it
increasingly difficult

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:23.980
to get any work done,

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:26.330
pay attention to your
physical surroundings.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.340
Improve upon your physical surroundings.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:30.770
Make sure that the space that you take up

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:32.970
is conducive to productivity.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:34.800
It's not just a nothing tip.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.360
It's a powerful psychological phenomenon

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.120
that we need to take advantage of

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.730
if we want to stay focused.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:43.710
Number five, purge your
digital surroundings.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.510
Probably even more important
in today's day and age

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:49.200
is making sure that your
digital surroundings

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.710
and your digital environment
that you're a part of

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:54.660
aren't begging you to
procrastinate every five seconds.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.070
And this can often be
more subtle and insidious

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:59.520
than it might look or seem on the surface.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:02.370
When you get a random email from Old Navy

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.220
that they're having a 40% off sale

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.420
even though you haven't
shopped there in six years,

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.860
or when you get a little
Facebook Messenger blip

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:11.940
on your phone from some group
chat that you're a part of

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.200
saying, "Hey, who wants
to hop on Apex right now?

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:16.190
Did you see the new
Halo Infinite trailer?"

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:18.750
These things might seem like nothing,

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:20.780
a little bit of a one-second distraction

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:21.980
and you can check it really quick

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:23.150
and then you can get back to work.

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:25.590
But the problem with
that is that quite often,

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.620
the most important work that
we have to do in our lives

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:33.200
requires deep, unbreaking
focus for several hours.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:36.640
And it is almost impossible
to reach that deep work state

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:38.700
when we're constantly
being pulled out of it.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.940
Our attention is constantly
being grabbed by notifications

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:44.490
from phone calls, from
text message sounds.

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:48.090
And if you have kind of an
ADHD-esque brain like mine,

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:49.470
that's a big deal.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.240
You will follow that notification,

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.390
you will see what someone
said on the group chat,

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:56.080
and you'll probably engage
with it for 10 to 15 seconds,

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:58.780
or even worse, you'll
go down a rabbit trail

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:01.650
and you'll spend 20 to
30 minutes on YouTube

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:04.540
even though you had absolutely
no plan on doing that.

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.910
So one of the most
important things you can do

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:09.290
to cure your procrastination

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:12.270
is to eliminate gateways
to procrastination.

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:13.310
And it even boils down

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:15.910
to the amount of desktop clutter you have,

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:17.460
or what's on your toolbar,

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:20.230
because every time your
phone buzzes in your pocket,

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:23.510
it's like Mark Zuckerberg
himself is tugging on your shirt,

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:24.710
telling you to pay attention

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.700
to what he wants you to pay attention to,

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:28.370
because whatever that notification is

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:30.540
is more important than
whatever you're doing.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:31.880
But you have to ask yourself,

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:34.810
are you the master of
your own life, or is Zuck?

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:38.380
Number six, stare at one
thing for 60 seconds.

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:40.300
So judging by the title of this tip,

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:41.287
you might be thinking that,

00:07:41.287 --> 00:07:42.880
"Wow, Joey is running out of content.

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:44.540
He's just trying to fluff up this video

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:45.950
and increase the watch time."

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:47.200
And you're totally right.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.390
I need more tips and this
video isn't long enough,

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:51.670
but also this tip seems
really interesting.

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:54.480
I tried it one time and it
instantly worked for me.

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:57.040
And I got this from Andrew
Huberman's Instagram.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:59.150
If you guys don't know,
Dr. Andrew Huberman

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:02.050
is a neuroscientist at
Stanford, I believe,

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:03.960
and he talked about how one way

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.520
to dramatically increase
focus in 60 seconds,

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:08.930
especially if you're already

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:11.600
in a distracted, chaotic mental state,

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:16.170
is to focus all of your attention
onto one specific target,

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:18.480
this could be one letter in one word,

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:21.450
at the distance that you
would be doing that work in

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:22.760
for 60 seconds.

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:25.120
Do not break that eye contact

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.330
and breathe deeply as you do it.

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:29.150
And at the end of that 60 seconds,

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:31.000
your focus collects itself.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:33.200
I think this is another
really good example

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:36.790
of how intimately linked our
psychology is to our physiology

00:08:36.790 --> 00:08:40.310
and how our actual eye
movement, not being chaotic,

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:42.920
collects our mental focus as well.

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:44.260
When we focus with our eyes,

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:46.000
we also focus with our brain.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.590
And what we do with our eyes also matters.

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:52.490
That's sort of the entire
concept of EMDR therapy.

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:54.060
You should look it up, super cool stuff.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:55.010
So give that a shot.

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:58.470
Focus on one specific
target for 60 seconds

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.387
while taking deep and relaxing breaths.

00:09:00.387 --> 00:09:02.580
And you'll notice that
your brain focuses too

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:03.830
and you can penetrate

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:06.350
through the resistance

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:08.360
in the way of doing the
thing you need to do.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:11.830
Okay, so tip number seven is
confront your distractions

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:12.810
before working.

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:15.020
Now, this is sort of
a direct contradiction

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:16.120
to rule number one,

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:18.120
which is why I saved it to near the end.

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.840
And I would kind of use this
technique as a last resort,

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:23.570
although it has been very
effective for me recently,

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:25.330
especially later in the day

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:27.140
when I've already eaten the frog

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:31.300
but I kind of have more frogs
to eat due to procrastination

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:32.920
and things piling up, et cetera.

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:36.800
The way this works is that
you set a timer for 15 minutes

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:39.180
or 10 minutes, or however long you need,

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:42.760
to actually engage with the
distractions and the escapes

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:44.170
that you want to engage with.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:45.750
Because so often what happens

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:48.460
is we don't wanna do this super hard thing

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:50.640
that we know we need to do, so we escape,

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:52.020
kind of impulsively.

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.220
We open the Word doc that
we're supposed to be on,

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:56.460
and then we click away and
then we just browse Instagram

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:57.530
and Facebook or whatever.

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.790
And you're sort of in this constant battle

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:02.100
of just dipping your toes
into the distraction,

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:03.600
dipping your toes into the work,

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:05.930
and it's just this seesaw that never ends.

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.560
And you actually never
have an intentional break

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:10.180
or an intentional work sesh.

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:11.950
So what I've been doing is setting a timer

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:13.710
for 15 or 20 minutes

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:17.030
and intentionally diving
into each distraction

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:19.080
that I want to do.

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:21.460
I say, "Okay, I'm checking Facebook now.

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:23.190
Right now, I'm intentionally seeing

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:24.470
if I have any notifications

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:25.830
or if I have any messages."

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:29.250
And I'm allowing myself
to mentally check that box

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:30.230
and clear it.

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:31.640
Then I move on to Instagram

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:33.630
and I say, "Okay, now I'm on Instagram.

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:35.160
I'm gonna have my way with Instagram.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:36.930
Do I have any message requests?

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:38.760
Who posted on their Stories?

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:40.570
Cool, I've done that now.

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:42.070
Now I can put it on the back burner."

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:43.170
And this might seem weird,

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:46.340
but I think the intentionality
is the difference here.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:48.600
When you intentionally
check the mental box

